  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1154 AM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nTODAY'S FLOOD RISK: \n\nAfter several days of heavy rainfall rates and reports of flash\nflooding and debris flows, we have decided to hoist a Flash \nFlood Watch for all of eastern Utah and western Colorado today \ninto this evening. The peak of anomalous moisture occurs today \nalong with a passing wave to tap into the moderate instability \nin place. Expect storms to continue to produce rainfall rates in\nexcess of 1-2 inches per hour with burn scars especially \nvulnerable. Models are still scouring out much of the convective\ndebris clouds through late morning. The eastern AZ wave arrives\nat peak heating to help release the instability and produce \nscattered to widespread convection. Storms today take on a bit \nmore northerly track which could also help it align on terrain \nwhich has been sheltered in the E/SE flow the past few days.\n\nSTORMS TODAY AND TONIGHT: \n\nThe main concern over the next 24 hours will be a weak \nshortwave tracking over portions of the area. The moisture we \nhave in place has already supported heavy rainfall, but the wave\nis going to bring a slight uptick (PWAT 175-215% of normal). It\nwill also supply some lift to aid convective development, which\nshould translate to widespread coverage. Given this \nenvironment, storms will be capable of producing strong winds, \nhail, and heavy rainfall. Models show the shortwave trough still\ntracking overhead tonight. This could support showers and \nstorms continuing overnight. Although by late evening, the \nrainfall rates should be on the decrease. \n\nLOOKING AHEAD: \n\nOn Thursday, the trough crosses the Divide leaving behind \nwesterly flow. Plenty of moisture will still be around for peak \nheating, so it terrain-driven storms are likely in the \nafternoon. Despite some dry air behind the wave, the moisture \nvalues still support the potential for heavy rainfall. \n\nOn Friday, northwest flow will be in place and models hint at \ndrier air trying to filter into the area. If that happens, \nconvective coverage will likely decrease, although low-level \nmoisture is usually slow to filter out. Another round of \nterrain-driven storms is possible in the afternoon. \n\nAfter that, the confidence lowers as models are not sure how to\nhandle the pool of moisture. How far south does it go and when \nwill return flow bring it back into the area? Is there enough \nlingering moisture to keep orographic showers going into the \nweekend? Stay tuned!",
